1
00:00:00,780 --> 00:00:03,080
Flip a coin a thousand times.

2
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:07,930
Count plus one for heads and minus one
for tails, and keep a running total.

3
00:00:08,060 --> 00:00:11,400
The total wanders. This walk ends at four.

4
00:00:11,420 --> 00:00:15,880
The next ends at twenty, and the
one after that at minus thirty-two.

5
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:20,170
Before the first flip, nobody
can say where a walk will finish.

6
00:00:21,433 --> 00:00:23,543
So ask a different question.

7
00:00:23,673 --> 00:00:28,853
Square each final total, so the sign no
longer matters, and average the squares

8
00:00:28,873 --> 00:00:33,493
over many walks. After a handful
of walks the average jumps around.

9
00:00:33,513 --> 00:00:38,903
After four thousand, it has settled
near one thousand: the number of steps.

10
00:00:39,193 --> 00:00:43,893
That is a fact about none of the
walks, and a fact about all of them.

11
00:00:43,913 --> 00:00:46,800
It does not say where
any single walk ends.

12
00:00:46,820 --> 00:00:49,783
It says how much room the
walks take up together.

13
00:00:51,133 --> 00:00:56,153
The October quasi-Riemann manuscript
rests on one estimate of this kind,

14
00:00:56,173 --> 00:01:00,473
for a family of sums that nobody
can compute one at a time.

15
00:01:00,493 --> 00:01:02,843
Spending it takes one paragraph.

16
00:01:02,893 --> 00:01:05,323
Proving it takes most of the paper.

17
00:01:05,533 --> 00:01:10,033
This film is about that estimate:
what it means, how it is spent,

18
00:01:10,053 --> 00:01:11,803
and how it is earned.

19
00:01:11,933 --> 00:01:14,793
This film explains how the arguments work.

20
00:01:14,813 --> 00:01:17,483
It does not certify that they are correct.

21
00:01:17,613 --> 00:01:21,803
That is the work of careful
readers, and it takes time.

22
00:01:30,967 --> 00:01:36,517
Start with the word. An energy,
in this film, is a sum of squares.

23
00:01:36,647 --> 00:01:40,197
Take a list of numbers,
square each one, and add.

24
00:01:40,327 --> 00:01:45,557
A square is never negative, so it
forgets the sign and keeps only the size.

25
00:01:45,767 --> 00:01:51,187
Draw each number as a square whose side
is its size, and the energy is the total

26
00:01:51,207 --> 00:01:52,277
area.

27
00:01:53,533 --> 00:01:56,523
Why squares, and not plain sizes?

28
00:01:56,573 --> 00:01:59,993
Because squares add up
over perpendicular pieces.

29
00:02:00,013 --> 00:02:04,953
That is Pythagoras: the square on the long
side equals the two squares on the short

30
00:02:04,973 --> 00:02:08,793
sides. Fourier analysis
obeys the same law.

31
00:02:08,813 --> 00:02:13,803
Split a signal into waves, and its energy
equals the total energy of the waves.

32
00:02:13,933 --> 00:02:18,713
That is Parseval’s identity, and it is why
physicists call the integral of a signal

33
00:02:18,733 --> 00:02:22,523
squared its energy. The word stuck.

34
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:26,580
Now square a sum of N terms.

35
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:30,260
The square spreads into a
grid of N squared products.

36
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:35,270
The N products on the diagonal are the
terms squared, and they are never negative.

37
00:02:35,480 --> 00:02:39,540
All the cancellation lives off
the diagonal, in the cross terms,

38
00:02:39,560 --> 00:02:42,070
where a plus can meet a minus.

39
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:46,070
For random signs, the cross
terms average out to nothing.

40
00:02:46,120 --> 00:02:51,700
Only the diagonal survives, so the energy
is about N, and a typical sum has size

41
00:02:51,720 --> 00:02:53,590
about the square root of N.

42
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:59,350
That is square-root cancellation, the
benchmark for every sum in this film.

43
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:04,470
Arithmetic is not random,
but it can look it.

44
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:08,820
Here is the Mertens function, the
running total of Möbius signs,

45
00:03:08,840 --> 00:03:13,990
computed to twelve hundred, and its
square beside the line y equals x.

46
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:15,990
A picture proves nothing.

47
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:20,980
A bound of x to the one half plus epsilon
for this function is equivalent to the

48
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:22,870
Riemann hypothesis.

49
00:03:24,133 --> 00:03:28,643
Now take a whole family of sums
and add the squares of all of them.

50
00:03:28,693 --> 00:03:33,363
An upper bound for that total is what
this film calls an energy budget.

51
00:03:33,493 --> 00:03:38,273
The manuscripts call it a mean-square
estimate, and the word energy appears in

52
00:03:38,293 --> 00:03:40,323
their labels and section titles.

53
00:03:40,533 --> 00:03:43,683
A budget says nothing
about any one member.

54
00:03:43,733 --> 00:03:46,323
An energy is a sum of squares.

55
00:03:46,373 --> 00:03:49,283
A budget is a promise about the sum.

56
00:03:53,367 --> 00:03:57,477
The sum the October proof
needs is called A one of D.

57
00:03:57,607 --> 00:04:02,067
It adds Möbius values, weighted
by a fixed character, nu,

58
00:04:02,087 --> 00:04:06,437
over the ideals of the Eisenstein
integers with norm about D.

59
00:04:06,567 --> 00:04:12,027
It has about D terms. The goal is to
show that it is much smaller than D,

60
00:04:12,047 --> 00:04:16,197
because enough cancellation in it
rules out zeros of an L-function.

61
00:04:16,327 --> 00:04:20,837
The Fixed Frontier explains why,
in its chapters two and six.

62
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:26,960
One sum on its own is hard, because
nothing controls its cross terms.

63
00:04:26,980 --> 00:04:32,720
A family is easier. Lay it out as a table:
each row is one member of the family,

64
00:04:32,740 --> 00:04:34,930
and each column is one term.

65
00:04:35,060 --> 00:04:39,650
The paper uses exactly these
words, rows and columns.

66
00:04:39,767 --> 00:04:42,277
Square every row and add.

67
00:04:42,327 --> 00:04:46,827
Swap the order of summation, and
each cross term pairs two columns,

68
00:04:46,847 --> 00:04:48,947
averaged over all the rows.

69
00:04:48,967 --> 00:04:53,107
If different columns are orthogonal,
like perpendicular directions,

70
00:04:53,127 --> 00:04:57,877
that average is small. The family
cancels the cross terms for you.

71
00:04:59,167 --> 00:05:02,157
This gives budgets a characteristic shape.

72
00:05:02,207 --> 00:05:06,827
The energy is at most the number
of rows plus the number of columns,

73
00:05:06,847 --> 00:05:10,827
times the energy of the
coefficients, up to tiny losses.

74
00:05:10,847 --> 00:05:14,077
The classical example is the large sieve.

75
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:17,350
And rows plus columns cannot be beaten.

76
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:21,300
Put all the weight on one
column, and every row sees it:

77
00:05:21,320 --> 00:05:23,750
the energy is the number of rows.

78
00:05:23,880 --> 00:05:27,380
Let one row be blind to the
differences between columns,

79
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:32,500
and that row alone collects them all:
the energy is the number of columns.

80
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:36,310
A budget of this shape is
as good as a budget gets.

81
00:05:37,567 --> 00:05:42,877
The paper’s family twists the target
by a sextic phase, chi n of u.

82
00:05:43,007 --> 00:05:49,147
The rows are Eisenstein integers u with
norm up to H, where H is D to the one

83
00:05:49,167 --> 00:05:51,597
plus theta, a little more than D.

84
00:05:51,807 --> 00:05:55,517
The columns are the ideals
n with norm about D.

85
00:05:55,647 --> 00:06:01,227
Proposition 3.1 says that the energy of
all the rows together is at most about

86
00:06:01,247 --> 00:06:03,037
D times H.

87
00:06:03,167 --> 00:06:09,147
There are about H rows, so a typical row
has energy about D and size about the

88
00:06:09,167 --> 00:06:14,107
square root of D. That is
square-root cancellation, on average.

89
00:06:14,127 --> 00:06:16,507
A budget is cheaper than a bound.

90
00:06:16,527 --> 00:06:21,757
You pay for it with orthogonality, and
you get it for the whole family at once.

91
00:06:23,033 --> 00:06:28,813
Here is a real family, computed at a
small scale: D equal to two thousand,

92
00:06:28,833 --> 00:06:34,413
with 15,528 rows and 847 columns.

93
00:06:34,433 --> 00:06:40,133
Each square is one row. No row stands
out: the largest is about three times the

94
00:06:40,153 --> 00:06:44,853
typical size. And the total comes within
five percent of what random signs would

95
00:06:44,873 --> 00:06:45,783
give.

96
00:06:45,980 --> 00:06:50,760
At a scale this small, that tests
nothing about the paper’s claim.

97
00:06:50,780 --> 00:06:54,410
It shows only what a budget
looks like when it holds.

98
00:06:58,567 --> 00:07:03,027
A family has two jobs, and they
pull in opposite directions.

99
00:07:03,047 --> 00:07:08,787
To prove a budget, the members must
differ: orthogonality is what pays for it.

100
00:07:08,807 --> 00:07:13,907
To use a budget on one sum, that sum
must appear in the family many times:

101
00:07:13,927 --> 00:07:16,037
the members must agree.

102
00:07:17,300 --> 00:07:19,650
The sextic phase does both.

103
00:07:19,780 --> 00:07:22,770
Fix a column n and let u run.

104
00:07:22,900 --> 00:07:28,530
Chi n of u is a sixth root of one,
or zero, and it changes with u.

105
00:07:28,660 --> 00:07:32,370
Different columns change
differently, so they are orthogonal.

106
00:07:32,420 --> 00:07:34,290
That is diversity.

107
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:38,910
Now set u equal to p to
the sixth, for a prime p.

108
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:42,990
A sixth root of one, raised
to the sixth power, is one.

109
00:07:43,120 --> 00:07:47,500
So every phase becomes one, except
on the terms that p divides,

110
00:07:47,520 --> 00:07:53,740
where it is zero. If p has norm about
Y, that row is the target minus about

111
00:07:53,760 --> 00:07:57,630
D over Y terms. That is repetition.

112
00:07:58,900 --> 00:08:01,730
The price of repetition is rarity.

113
00:08:01,780 --> 00:08:04,560
Copies sit only at sixth powers.

114
00:08:04,580 --> 00:08:10,480
For p to the sixth to be a row, the norm
of p can be at most H to the one sixth.

115
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:15,040
Call that Y. So the family holds
about Y copies of the target,

116
00:08:15,060 --> 00:08:18,130
up to a logarithm, not H of them.

117
00:08:18,267 --> 00:08:22,537
Here is some of this film’s own
arithmetic, not a claim from the paper.

118
00:08:22,667 --> 00:08:28,247
If the twist had order k instead of
six, the copies would sit at p to the k,

119
00:08:28,267 --> 00:08:31,607
and the same spending would
give the exponent on the dial.

120
00:08:31,627 --> 00:08:37,287
At k equal to six, it is eleven
twelfths, plus five theta over twelve.

121
00:08:37,307 --> 00:08:41,257
Lower orders give more
copies and smaller exponents.

122
00:08:41,447 --> 00:08:46,227
At the bottom of the dial, with no twist
at all, every row would be nearly the

123
00:08:46,247 --> 00:08:50,787
target, and a budget of the same shape
would give the Riemann hypothesis for this

124
00:08:50,807 --> 00:08:56,707
L-function. But that family has no
orthogonality, and nobody can prove its budget.

125
00:08:56,727 --> 00:09:01,507
The sixth power is where provable
meets useful: diverse enough to prove,

126
00:09:01,527 --> 00:09:03,717
repetitive enough to spend.

127
00:09:07,767 --> 00:09:09,907
Here is how the budget is spent.

128
00:09:09,927 --> 00:09:15,507
Take every primary prime p with
norm between Y over two and Y.

129
00:09:15,527 --> 00:09:20,547
Landau’s prime ideal theorem says
there are about Y over log Y of them.

130
00:09:20,567 --> 00:09:24,787
Call that number J. Their
sixth powers are distinct rows,

131
00:09:24,807 --> 00:09:30,117
and each of those rows is the target,
up to an error of about D over Y.

132
00:09:31,400 --> 00:09:33,620
The budget covers every row.

133
00:09:33,640 --> 00:09:36,710
Keep only the J copies and drop the rest.

134
00:09:36,840 --> 00:09:42,260
That is allowed because every row contributes
a square, and squares are never negative,

135
00:09:42,280 --> 00:09:45,140
so what is left is at most the whole.

136
00:09:45,160 --> 00:09:49,350
You may drop any part of an energy,
because every part is a square.

137
00:09:49,480 --> 00:09:53,350
What you keep is the
target, many times over.

138
00:09:54,633 --> 00:09:59,333
For each copy, the target squared
is at most twice the copy squared,

139
00:09:59,353 --> 00:10:01,383
plus twice the error squared.

140
00:10:01,513 --> 00:10:03,703
Average over the J copies.

141
00:10:03,753 --> 00:10:08,533
The target squared is at most two
over J times the energy of the copies,

142
00:10:08,553 --> 00:10:13,623
plus the error squared. And the energy
of the copies is at most the budget.

143
00:10:13,813 --> 00:10:18,033
Put in the numbers. The budget
is D to the two plus theta.

144
00:10:18,053 --> 00:10:23,073
Dividing by J removes a factor
of about Y, the sixth root of H.

145
00:10:23,093 --> 00:10:27,953
What remains is D to the eleven
sixths, plus five theta over six.

146
00:10:27,973 --> 00:10:32,113
The error term is D to the five
thirds, minus theta over three,

147
00:10:32,133 --> 00:10:35,833
which is smaller. Take the
square root: eleven twelfths,

148
00:10:35,853 --> 00:10:38,113
plus five theta over twelve.

149
00:10:38,133 --> 00:10:40,323
Then let theta be small.

150
00:10:41,600 --> 00:10:44,030
There is a second way to spend a budget.

151
00:10:44,080 --> 00:10:50,780
If the total is at most B, then at most
B over T squared rows can exceed T,

152
00:10:50,800 --> 00:10:53,710
because each of them alone uses T squared.

153
00:10:53,760 --> 00:10:57,820
Counting outliers this way is
how zero-density estimates work,

154
00:10:57,840 --> 00:11:01,660
and it is how the September
manuscript spends its own estimates,

155
00:11:01,680 --> 00:11:03,710
as the last chapter shows.

156
00:11:07,767 --> 00:11:10,757
Now the hard part: earning the budget.

157
00:11:10,967 --> 00:11:15,587
Nobody can compute these sums one
by one, so the proof never does.

158
00:11:15,607 --> 00:11:18,387
It transforms the energy instead.

159
00:11:18,407 --> 00:11:23,507
A Fourier transform keeps the total
energy and scrambles everything else.

160
00:11:23,527 --> 00:11:28,147
That is Plancherel’s theorem, and it is
why the proof works with energies rather

161
00:11:28,167 --> 00:11:30,037
than with single sums.

162
00:11:31,300 --> 00:11:37,040
Expand the squares. Each cross
term pairs two columns, n and m,

163
00:11:37,060 --> 00:11:41,800
and sums the product of their phases
over all the rows u in a disk.

164
00:11:41,820 --> 00:11:47,080
Poisson summation rewrites a sum over
positions as a sum over frequencies.

165
00:11:47,100 --> 00:11:52,690
The sum over u becomes a sum over
a new variable, h, the dual row.

166
00:11:52,800 --> 00:11:54,750
And the row range flips.

167
00:11:54,960 --> 00:11:59,020
H rows become about D
squared over H dual rows.

168
00:11:59,040 --> 00:12:01,100
Call that number script H.

169
00:12:01,120 --> 00:12:07,390
With H equal to D to the one plus theta,
script H is D to the one minus theta.

170
00:12:07,520 --> 00:12:11,420
The two row ranges always
multiply to D squared.

171
00:12:11,440 --> 00:12:14,190
Now there are fewer rows than columns.

172
00:12:15,467 --> 00:12:20,727
The Fourier transform of a sextic
phase is a Gauss sum: roots of one,

173
00:12:20,747 --> 00:12:22,967
weighted by waves, added up.

174
00:12:22,987 --> 00:12:27,847
For ordinary integers there is a classical
identity: the square of a quadratic

175
00:12:27,867 --> 00:12:30,087
Gauss sum is just a sign.

176
00:12:30,107 --> 00:12:35,097
The paper uses a sextic relative
of it, after Hasse and Heath-Brown.

177
00:12:35,233 --> 00:12:41,053
It says that the Möbius value times a
sextic Gauss sum equals a cubic Gauss sum,

178
00:12:41,073 --> 00:12:43,103
up to factors of size one.

179
00:12:43,233 --> 00:12:45,743
The Möbius function has been absorbed.

180
00:12:45,873 --> 00:12:51,183
Checked here at twenty-two primes, the two
sides agree to fourteen decimal places.

181
00:12:51,313 --> 00:12:57,453
What is left is a new family, B h of
X: cubic Gauss sums as coefficients,

182
00:12:57,473 --> 00:13:02,863
twisted by phases in h, up to fixed
twists that the outline suppresses.

183
00:13:04,133 --> 00:13:08,913
Schematically, the original
energy is at most D times H,

184
00:13:08,933 --> 00:13:14,113
plus H over D times the energy of
the new family over the dual rows.

185
00:13:14,133 --> 00:13:19,793
So the budget now asks for this:
the energy of B, over script H rows,

186
00:13:19,813 --> 00:13:22,003
at most about D squared.

187
00:13:22,213 --> 00:13:25,203
Poisson summation does
not shrink the energy.

188
00:13:25,253 --> 00:13:30,113
It moves it to a side where the rows are
short and the coefficients are Gauss sums,

189
00:13:30,133 --> 00:13:31,603
which have a theory.

190
00:13:35,667 --> 00:13:38,337
Cubic Gauss sums are not random.

191
00:13:38,387 --> 00:13:43,007
Kubota and Patterson showed that they are
the Fourier coefficients of a cubic theta

192
00:13:43,027 --> 00:13:47,967
function, an automorphic form on
three-dimensional hyperbolic space.

193
00:13:47,987 --> 00:13:53,057
Automorphic forms have symmetry, and
symmetry gives a transformation law.

194
00:13:53,167 --> 00:13:58,507
The law wants every index the theta
function contains: each square-free n,

195
00:13:58,527 --> 00:14:02,237
and also n times b cubed, for every b.

196
00:14:02,367 --> 00:14:06,427
The family B h has only
the square-free indices.

197
00:14:06,447 --> 00:14:10,987
So the proof completes the sum, adding
the cube terms, to make a new sum,

198
00:14:11,007 --> 00:14:14,507
T h of X. The rows are unchanged.

199
00:14:14,527 --> 00:14:18,477
The cube terms are borrowed, and
chapter seven pays them back.

200
00:14:19,767 --> 00:14:22,387
Then the transformation acts.

201
00:14:22,407 --> 00:14:29,107
A long sum over n, with norm about X, becomes
a shorter dual sum over a new variable,

202
00:14:29,127 --> 00:14:32,197
m. And the twist changes character.

203
00:14:32,327 --> 00:14:37,907
At each prime dividing h, the Fourier
side contributed chi to the minus one,

204
00:14:37,927 --> 00:14:41,557
and the theta side contributes
chi to the minus two.

205
00:14:41,747 --> 00:14:47,047
Together that is chi to the minus
three, which equals chi cubed.

206
00:14:47,067 --> 00:14:51,167
And a sixth root of one,
cubed, is plus or minus one.

207
00:14:51,187 --> 00:14:53,567
The twist is now quadratic.

208
00:14:53,587 --> 00:14:59,327
A sixth-order twist, cubed, is a sign,
and signs are what the sharpest sieve can

209
00:14:59,347 --> 00:15:00,257
price.

210
00:15:01,533 --> 00:15:06,313
Goldmakher and Louvel’s quadratic
large sieve, building on Heath-Brown,

211
00:15:06,333 --> 00:15:11,673
gives rows plus columns, up to tiny
losses: the shape from chapter two,

212
00:15:11,693 --> 00:15:15,993
now paying the bill. The completed
energy comes in at script H,

213
00:15:16,013 --> 00:15:18,873
plus script H squared over X.

214
00:15:18,893 --> 00:15:22,123
At the basic scales,
that is within budget.

215
00:15:23,400 --> 00:15:25,510
Why insist on quadratic?

216
00:15:25,640 --> 00:15:31,380
For a family with M rows and L columns, the
general sieve for higher-order characters

217
00:15:31,400 --> 00:15:35,940
carries an extra term: M L
together, to the two thirds.

218
00:15:35,960 --> 00:15:41,060
Here the rows are D to the one minus theta,
and the dual columns are D to the one

219
00:15:41,080 --> 00:15:46,470
minus two theta. Rows plus columns
is about D to the one minus theta.

220
00:15:46,600 --> 00:15:50,740
The extra term is D to the
four thirds, minus two theta,

221
00:15:50,760 --> 00:15:55,190
larger by a power of D. With
it, the count would fail.

222
00:15:59,267 --> 00:16:03,247
Completion borrowed the cube
terms, and they must be returned.

223
00:16:03,267 --> 00:16:08,327
They cannot simply be dropped, because
contributions from different cubes can cancel.

224
00:16:08,347 --> 00:16:13,807
Möbius inversion returns them exactly:
the sum the proof needs is a weighted sum

225
00:16:13,827 --> 00:16:18,887
of completed sums, at the smaller
column scales X over the norm of d,

226
00:16:18,907 --> 00:16:19,937
cubed.

227
00:16:21,200 --> 00:16:25,820
For small d, Cauchy–Schwarz and
the completed bound pay directly,

228
00:16:25,840 --> 00:16:28,190
up to a cutoff, H sub c.

229
00:16:28,400 --> 00:16:32,910
At the basic scales, the cutoff
is D to the two theta over three.

230
00:16:33,040 --> 00:16:36,430
Here theta earns its
keep for the first time.

231
00:16:36,567 --> 00:16:42,467
Large d leave remainders: energies of
the same kind, at a shorter column scale,

232
00:16:42,487 --> 00:16:47,227
L sub b, equal to X over
the norm of b, cubed.

233
00:16:47,247 --> 00:16:51,717
But the rows have not shrunk, and
the bound still required is X.

234
00:16:51,847 --> 00:16:54,117
That is not progress yet.

235
00:16:55,367 --> 00:16:58,867
So the proof applies Poisson
summation twice more.

236
00:16:58,887 --> 00:17:05,107
The first, in h, produces new rows,
y, with norm up to L sub b squared

237
00:17:05,127 --> 00:17:09,747
over script H. Then it deliberately
enlarges that row range,

238
00:17:09,767 --> 00:17:14,757
which is allowed because every term is a
square, and squares are never negative.

239
00:17:14,887 --> 00:17:18,677
It is the same positivity
that let the budget be spent.

240
00:17:18,800 --> 00:17:22,420
The second Poisson summation
brings the rows back shorter,

241
00:17:22,440 --> 00:17:25,550
and the coefficients
return to cubic Gauss sums.

242
00:17:25,760 --> 00:17:31,100
Both scales have shrunk by the norm of
b cubed, and their ratio is unchanged.

243
00:17:31,120 --> 00:17:33,470
This is the transfer estimate.

244
00:17:34,733 --> 00:17:37,243
Now the bound runs as a recursion.

245
00:17:37,293 --> 00:17:40,873
The energy at one level, measured
against its column scale,

246
00:17:40,893 --> 00:17:44,843
is at most one plus the
same measure one level down.

247
00:17:44,973 --> 00:17:50,073
Because the norm of b exceeds the cutoff,
each level shrinks the rows by at least

248
00:17:50,093 --> 00:17:52,603
a factor of D to the two theta.

249
00:17:52,813 --> 00:17:56,553
After a bounded number of
levels, depending only on theta,

250
00:17:56,573 --> 00:18:01,643
the row parameter is at most one,
and plain counting finishes the job.

251
00:18:01,904 --> 00:18:06,787
The full proof carries extra twists,
common factors, and dyadic ranges through

252
00:18:06,807 --> 00:18:10,747
every step, by Heath-Brown’s
admissible-exponent method.

253
00:18:10,767 --> 00:18:12,467
But the skeleton is this.

254
00:18:12,487 --> 00:18:15,267
The proof never pays the energy outright.

255
00:18:15,287 --> 00:18:19,347
It pays a bounded number of
instalments, each at a smaller scale,

256
00:18:19,367 --> 00:18:22,117
and the last one is small enough to count.

257
00:18:26,200 --> 00:18:30,260
Every step had a price. Theta is the fuel.

258
00:18:30,280 --> 00:18:34,820
It makes the dual rows shorter than the
columns, the positive gap that the iteration

259
00:18:34,840 --> 00:18:38,230
needs, and it makes the recursion shrink.

260
00:18:38,280 --> 00:18:42,900
It costs five theta over twelve in
the exponent, so it is kept small,

261
00:18:42,920 --> 00:18:48,980
but never zero. Epsilon is the friction:
every sieve loses a tiny power,

262
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:52,790
and a bounded number of steps
loses a bounded number of them.

263
00:18:54,067 --> 00:18:58,337
The September manuscript runs
the same idea at a larger scale.

264
00:18:58,387 --> 00:19:02,047
Its reflected-energy bounds
control completed theta rows,

265
00:19:02,067 --> 00:19:05,887
and two moment bounds, each
proved by its own recursion,

266
00:19:05,907 --> 00:19:09,137
limit how many rows a
zero detector can flag.

267
00:19:09,267 --> 00:19:13,967
A budget spent by counting outliers,
with several accounts open at once.

268
00:19:13,987 --> 00:19:18,607
Keep theta positive and small, pay
epsilon a bounded number of times,

269
00:19:18,627 --> 00:19:20,497
and the ledger closes.

270
00:19:22,007 --> 00:19:28,227
So, an energy budget. An energy is
a sum of squares, and a budget is

271
00:19:28,247 --> 00:19:31,880
a promise about the total: a
fact about none of the members,

272
00:19:31,900 --> 00:19:33,957
and a fact about all of them.

273
00:19:34,067 --> 00:19:40,047
It can be proved, because transforms preserve
energy and one sharp inequality prices

274
00:19:40,067 --> 00:19:45,937
it. It is useful, because arithmetic plants
copies of the target inside the family.

275
00:19:46,067 --> 00:19:52,977
And it is spent by the one rule every square
obeys: no part of it is ever negative.

276
00:19:53,100 --> 00:19:55,960
This film explains how the arguments work.

277
00:19:55,980 --> 00:19:58,650
It does not certify that they are correct.

278
00:19:58,780 --> 00:20:02,970
That is the work of careful
readers, and it takes time.
